<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Ayyubid Emirate of Homs</h1><p class="st-meta">1179 CE – 1262 CE · capital Homs</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Ayyubid principality of the descendants of Shirkuh, whose last prince helped defeat the Mongols at the first battle of Homs in 1260.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1179 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a> to his cousin Nasir al-Din Muhammad, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirkuh">Shirkuh</a>, the uncle who had conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The town remained with this line of the family as a hereditary principality.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a> guarded the gap between the coast and the interior and faced the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-tripoli">County of Tripoli</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizari</a> castles. Asad al-Din <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirkuh">Shirkuh</a> II ruled for over fifty years (1186-1240), keeping the town through shifting alliances. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mansur-ibrahim">al-Mansur Ibrahim</a> fought alongside the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/franks">Franks</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and lost at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-la-forbie">La Forbie</a> in 1244. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ashraf-musa-emir-of-homs">Al-Ashraf Musa</a> lost Homs for a time to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ashraf-musa-emir-of-homs">Al-Ashraf Musa</a>, restored by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, switched sides and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> troops defeated a Mongol army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a> in December 1260. After his death in 1262, the Mamluk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> annexed the principality.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1179 CE – 1262 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.16%;width:1.65%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1179 CE – 1262 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Homs</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Shirkuh II, al-Mansur Ibrahim, al-Ashraf Musa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ayyubids of Homs</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1220&amp;focus=ayyubid-emirate-of-homs" aria-label="Ayyubid Emirate of Homs on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5612 921 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ayyubid Emirate of Homs lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5932 1177L5811 1337M5932 1177L5815 1335"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5811 1337h0M5815 1335h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5932 1177h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5932 1177h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Homs. <a href="/atlas?year=1220&amp;focus=ayyubid-emirate-of-homs">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

